The one gap commercial products hold is measurement authority. Calibration is a hardware and certification problem, not a software problem. The system makes no claims about detector accuracy — it faithfully processes whatever the attached transducer reports.

5. The Distinguishing Feature

Mainstream radiation software asks: "What is the current dose rate?"

CTW Geiger Scope asks: "What happened, when, in what pattern, how often has it happened before, and what does the temporal structure of the signal reveal?"

The recurrence subsystem is the clearest expression of this. No commercial product in the surveyed field maintains a searchable ledger of CPS event signatures and scores new events against it for recurrence. That is a forensic capability, not a monitoring capability.

6. Formal System Classification

CTW Geiger Scope is a radiation signal forensic console implementing a seven-layer pipeline: Acquisition → Stable Display Conditioning → Rolling Statistical Analysis → Event Detection & Classification → Evidentiary Ledger Maintenance → Multi-modal Visualization → Threshold Alerting.

It is designed for anomaly characterization and temporal pattern forensics on radiation detector output streams, with secondary capability in real-time monitoring and dose tracking.

It is not a spectrometer. It is not a mapping platform. It is a forensic signal analysis system that happens to consume radiation detector output.
